    {
      "slug": "reddit-not-an-employee",
      "script": "She grabbed the wrong employee? A woman grabbed my arm in the paint aisle because I was wearing a red hoodie. The store uniform was red too, so apparently employment is color-coded. I told her twice I didn't work there. She called me a liar, then flagged down an actual employee in an orange vest. He looked at my hoodie, hers too, and asked which one of us needed a stepladder. She pointed at me. I said neither of us worked there. He laughed so hard he had to lean on a shelf, then paged security over the intercom for an aisle nine altercation, both parties in red. Store cameras caught the whole thing. Corporate posted the clip internally for de-escalation training: her face blurred and mine left in, on purpose. Which red hoodie became the training video?",
      "editor_note": "Punched up to a five-word question hook, tightened the mistaken-employee escalation, added one dry color-coded aside, and landed on the red-hoodie training video while preserving every fact and shot phrase."
    },
    {
      "slug": "reddit-thermostat-admin",
      "script": "Who froze the office? She controlled the office thermostat from an app on her phone and kept it at sixty two degrees to “stay sharp.” Everyone else just stayed cold. When anyone touched the wall panel, she blamed the app. Small problem: I'd installed the thermostat and still had admin access. Then a temp near the vent got blamed and written up for tampering, so I pulled the access log instead. Every override for four months straight was timestamped and tied to her phone—the same four months facilities quietly replaced two malfunctioning units at company expense. HR read the log twice. The temp got the write up removed and an apology. She got a very different meeting. The thermostat is now locked at a very reasonable seventy one degrees. How sharp did that access log feel?",
      "editor_note": "Punched up to a four-word question hook, compressed the thermostat cover story, added one dry admin-access turn, and landed on the access-log reversal while preserving every fact and shot phrase."
    }
  ],
